  "text": "2003A04 Outline the potential\nproblems associated with additives used to make medicines suitable\nfor intravenous injection.\nList:\n·\nTable: problem, drug, culprit,\npurpose\n·\nLogistical problems\nIngredient\nproblems:\nDrug\nPurpose\nProblem\nSodium metabisulfite\nLignocaine, adrenaline\nAnti-oxidant, anti-microbial\n·\nAnaphylaxis\nEgg lecithin\nPropofol\nEmulsifier\n·\n?Anaphylaxis\n·\nProtein deplete; theoretical risk\nBenzethonium chloride\nKetamine\nAntimicrobial\n·\n?Neurotoxicity\nPropylene glycol\nEtomidate, diazepam, digoxin,\nphenytoin\nSolubiliser\n·\nPain on injection\n·\nThrombophlebitis\nSoybean oil\nPropofol\nSolubiliser\n·\nLipotoxicity\n·\nBacterial contamination risk\n·\nPropofol infusion syndrome\nMannitol\nParacetamol\nTonicity\n·\nOsmotic diuresis\nGlycine\nRemifentanil\nBulk agent\n·\nNeurotoxic (not for intrathecal use)\n↑pH,\n↑concentration\nThiopentone\n·\nPrecipitation if intra-arterial\ninjection\n·\nNecrosis if extravasation\n·\nPrecipitation with acidic drugs e.g.\nsuxamethonium\nLogistical\nproblems:\n·\nShelf life\n·\nCost\nFeedback welcome at\nketaminenightmares@gmail.com",
